it's now thought that more than 50  people were killed by a russian missile  attack on a train station in the east of  ukraine on friday as they tried to  escape the fighting  the situation in the south and east of  the country is increasingly desperate as  russian forces focus on the area with  ukraine saying they're trying to destroy  civilians  the red cross again sought to reach the  besieged city of mariopol this week as  people try to flee  all as more atrocities are discovered  around cave  now  pascal hunt leads the international  committee of the red cross in ukraine  and joins us now live from there  good morning uh mr hunt um  you're in western ukraine this morning  uh can you tell us uh anything about the  situation uh for those who are fleeing  the fighting  yeah good morning  yes indeed the situation for those  fleeing the fighting wherever it happens  it's an extremely difficult situation  fighting is taking place in  densely populated area  in cities  and these cities are being shelled and  we have seen the images we all these  images are really shocking even  outrageous  so you can imagine the level of despair  for these people  they're trying to leave they wait for  the train  they are being shelled so this is really  a dramatic situation  well you're referring to the uh  explosion  uh the missile attack frankly on the  railway station at kramertorsk what um  what are your people there telling you  about the aftermath of that  many uh consequences people are  even more in fear and despair this is  one of the problem  they do not dare to take the train  anymore so they are moving by car  they have difficulty to reach the the  hospital as well and as i speak the  international committee of the red cross  together with the german red cross we're  also trying to facilitate the evacuation  of people wounded uh  in the front line so yes the situation  is is even more difficult for the people  they were trying to leave and  and this is becoming really difficult  and we see them moving more and more uh  and taking the private cars and moving  by roads  oh so people there are now distrusting  uh rail as a mode of travel which  presumably must make  evacuation even more difficult and even  slower  i don't know if they are distrusting the  the the railway but can you imagine you  are waiting in a railway station and  then uh the shelling is happening so  they are really afraid so what we have  seen is that some of them were moving by  car maybe they will try again to reach  another railway station and continue to  move by train but i confirm that people  continue to flee the area yes  you also have uh  teams uh who have been trying to get to  mario paul um can you tell us if any of  your people  actually made it there and whether or  not they made it uh what you understand  has happened there now  our team tried for five days  to go to mario paul  and it's a difficult journey because you  have to cross the front line you have to  go through many many checkpoints  sometimes the people at the checkpoint  and the soldiers they're not aware of  your or  of your trip  and it's just an illustration on how  difficult it gets to have translated  into action you know the agreement  between the capitals and how difficult  it is for the order to tickle down at  the checkpoint so we went there with  buses  we moved we try we try again and again  but unfortunately the situation around  mario paul was too dangerous the  security conditions were not good uh the  security guarantees we got were were not  good either so at around 20 kilometers  from mario paul  we had to go back  we collected people on the road we  collected people in berlin  it was a long journey difficult journey  private cars were joining our convoy  under the flag of the red cross and  finally more than thousand people reach  zapurige  it may sound as a drop in the ocean  because the needs are much bigger the  situation is difficult there  people are there with no food no water  no heating system so yeah  it's really held there so i think many  more of such operation will need to take  place in in the coming days in the  coming weeks it is urgent  and we really  are in contact with the russian  authorities with ukrainian authorities  to ensure that safe passage is being  provided to to these people so they move  they can move freely and safely to to  much safer place  many of us the red cross itself has come  under some criticism uh internationally  and indeed from ukrainian politicians  uh themselves in fact one spoke about it  on this uh program in previous weeks  saying that you hadn't done enough to  protect refugees  and that you are too close  to russia  to do the job  that you're asked to do properly how do  you respond to that  i think it's a good illustration of the  difficulties  uh of being neutral there you know many  people understand  the difficulty of neutrality as of  either you are with us or you are  against us and we have heard that many  years and i think neutrality is more  than  taking side and taking side of the  victims and doing everything we can  in order to be close to the victims but  for doing so we need to discuss with the  parties to the conflict we need to  discuss with the ukrainian authorities  we need to discuss with the russians  ultimately they are responsible  of the behavior of the troops on the  ground they are responsible for  providing safety and security to the  population and they are responsible for  allowing humanitarian actors to go to  the front line to cross frontline  we will continue to do so but it's a  complex endeavor and we are in  discussion with the parties on that well  there's no question about the the icrc's  international reputation but you rightly  raise the issue of neutrality  um  how is it possible to be neutral  when it is pretty clear  that one side  is committing  uh  war crimes  and uh seems ready to commit further war  crimes as long as neutrality is  understood  as a mean to reach the victim  as a position of defending the victims  and being close to them  and  as a mean to achieve  more protection and assistance  then  i think it is easier to understand that  and our neutral position  was  we have positive example of that it's  because of being neutral that we were  able to cross front line and to try to  go towards mariupol  it's because we are neutral that we can  go  to front lines and hot places  so  the meaningfulness of neutrality  is translating into  concrete outcome for the population and  we will continue to do that despite the  criticism  but i have to admit that the criticism  we faced with respect to not doing  enough or  to favor one side or the other  is also preventing us to work  it is undermining our mandate it is  undermining our access to people  and i hope that this will stop because  this is not helping us to do our work  here  can you give an example of where that  happened presumably you mean  that in some circumstances  uh trust is broken down presumably with  ukrainians uh uh in the  uh red cross and that's making it  difficult for you to  rescue survivors to provide uh medical  and other help  and this is not about the ukrainian  authorities with whom we are in contact  every day and they perfectly understand  our position it's more  difficult to understand for the public  for the diaspora  where basically they don't understand  that we have to discuss with the  russians  so it's a matter of us being better to  explain what we are doing  and also in being better in  showcasing what what we are doing on the  ground and to demonstrate that when  people are receiving aid and assistance  this is also due to the neutrality of  the red cross  all right then if i may just finally ask  you about we talked about mario paul um  and we've seen a lot of what's been  happening in the south but we've also  seen some  terrible uh reports  of what has happened in the north around  kiev and for example in butcher  what is uh the icrc scene there  we have seen the same image  as everyone  we have been in bucha in irpil in  norgamel yesterday we were in chile  north of the country  and we see  basically the same image everywhere and  this is truly  appalling and heartbreaking we are  discussing with the people there we find  people in total despair  with no food no electricity no water no  eating system they have to go out in the  streets to make some  fire  and cook you know  these people were living in in  horrible conditions  so this is  the level of suffering that we are  seeing is just immense  and i don't recall having seen that in  recent history  we are collecting information we are  discussing with the people we do our  utmost to provide them with assistance  and whenever we see  uh  problems  uh  concerned  then we discuss as well directly with  ukrainians and with the russians on that  this is part of our bilateral dialogue  with the parties  to remind them of the  obligation under the geneva convention  and this is complementary to  the job that is done by  other groups that are coming vocal on  that journalists that are doing a good  job on the ground and we are  complementing this work  and we are discussing with the russians  on that as well  mr hon thank you so much for talking to  us this morning  you're welcome thank you very much
 

